      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>scale is a fundamental betrayal of the social contract is often the more philosophically robust position to take in a seminar setting. \n\nThe reasoning is that the social contract is predicated on the protection of individual autonomy and privacy from the state; once the state uses its power to monitor its citizens' private lives without explicit, granular consent, it has effectively moved from being the protector to being a potential predator. Most core theorists would argue that 'security' is a means to protect those rights, not a justification to dismantle them. \n\nIf you use that as the thesis, you can argue that 'evolution' is just a euphemism for the erosion of the very rights that make the contract worth having in the first place. Does that sound like a solid enough angle for the lecture?",
